WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.180
Can the protein that detects a smell also tell its axon where to go?

2
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:14.380
In 1991, Linda Buck and Richard Axel found the odorant receptor genes.

3
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:20.833
In the nose, the rule is one neuron, one receptor, with rare exceptions.

4
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:26.933
Neurons with the same receptor are scattered within a zone of the nose.

5
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:33.333
Yet their axons gather in a few glomeruli in the olfactory bulb.

6
00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:39.907
A glomerulus is a ball of neuropil, where sensory axons meet bulb neurons.

7
00:00:42.217 --> 00:00:48.417
In 1996, Peter Mombaerts and colleagues marked axons from neurons using the P2

8
00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:49.417
promoter.

9
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:55.827
They converged on two glomeruli in each bulb, one medial, one lateral.

10
00:00:56.707 --> 00:00:58.827
One receptor, about two glomeruli.

11
00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:03.887
The receptor protein also reaches the axon terminals.

12
00:01:04.847 --> 00:01:05.947
Could it be part of the address?

13
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.380
Predict. Delete the P2 receptor, but keep the marker.

14
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.440
Where do the axons go?

15
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.140
They wandered, and failed to form a P2 glomerulus.

16
00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.200
So the receptor is needed.

17
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.320
A caveat: neurons without a working receptor often switch on a second receptor gene.

18
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:43.633
Predict. Put the M12 receptor in place of P2.

19
00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:45.733
Where do the axons go now?

20
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:57.033
They converged near the P2 glomerulus, and far from M12's own.

21
00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:02.737
So the receptor helps choose, and other features of the neuron count too.

22
00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.450
Here the shared label is the receptor itself.

23
00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.590
Unlike Sidekick, it is not known to bind matching receptors on other axons.

24
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:22.963
In layers and in self-avoidance, a match says same, and the cell decides what same

25
00:02:22.963 --> 00:02:28.503
means. Smell shares the logic of a shared label, but no receptor-to-receptor match

26
00:02:28.503 --> 00:02:32.623
is known; how the receptor sorts axons is still being worked out.

27
00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:40.127
Takeaway: in smell, receptor identity helps axons of one channel converge.

28
00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.807
Other features of the neuron count too.
